---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site.config';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="My parents bought a hand-built 286 in the 1990s. This is how that turned into a career in software, speech recognition, and large language models."
>
  <section class="page-heading section-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">About / John Vicondoa</p>
    <h1>A sheepherder's son finds a computer</h1>
  </section>

  <section class="section-shell about-shell">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        In the 1990s, my parents saw Bill Gates say on television that his goal
        was a computer on a desk in every home. Not long after that, they bought
        a hand-built 286 from a friend and put it on a desk in our house.
      </p>
      <p>
        It ran MS-DOS and had almost nothing installed on it. My parents were
        both blue-collar workers, and neither of them knew what to do with it,
        so it mostly sat idle. I was fascinated by it, but I had no frame of
        reference for how to use it or even how to learn. I would open the
        command prompt and type natural-language commands such as <code
          >let me write a paper</code
        > or <code>how are you doing?</code> The computer always answered the same
        way: <code>command not found</code>. I opened the case and stared at the
        technology inside, mesmerized by a machine I could not yet understand.
      </p>
      <p>
        That changed when one of my mom's coworkers came over. She was the first
        person I ever saw actually use the computer. She installed software she
        had purchased herself, including WordPerfect and several games. In her
        bag, I spotted a game called <em>Leather Goddesses of Phobos</em>. It
        was an Infocom text adventure, although I did not know that at the time.
        I was around fourteen years old, and the title sounded incredibly dirty,
        so naturally I had to play it.
      </p>
      <p>My mom's friend noticed my interest and gave me a challenge.</p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          Johnny, I'm going to hide this game on the computer. If you can find
          it, you can play it.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        She also left behind a copy of <em>DOS for Dummies</em>. My mission was
        on.
      </p>
      <p>
        I read that book page by page. There was no internet available to me,
        and it was the first real instruction I had ever received on how to use
        a computer. Eventually, I found the game. I was victorious, even though
        it turned out to be much less dirty than its name suggested. That
        challenge started a lifelong fascination, and what became one of my
        deepest special interests: computers.
      </p>
      <p>
        I bought a book about programming in BASIC and began writing my own
        software. Some of my earliest programs were launchers that let me type
        commands in something closer to natural language, much like I had tried
        to do the first time I sat in front of that MS-DOS prompt. Later, I
        taught myself Perl. I dialed into BBSs with a modem. I tried to learn
        everything I could about this wonderful machine and the world it opened
        up.
      </p>
      <p>
        When I finally got an internet account, I quickly found ways to get into
        trouble with it. I discovered that I could crash Microsoft Comic Chat
        clients using CTCP floods. What I did not realize was that I was also
        crashing the servers behind them. Microsoft was not very happy about
        that, and my internet account was shut down.
      </p>
      <p>
        That was also when I began finding communities I had never been able to
        find where I grew up. I found 2600, other teenage mischief-makers, and
        the San Francisco rave scene. The internet connected me to people who
        shared my curiosity, my interests, and my appetite for exploring systems
        that were not always meant to be explored.
      </p>
      <h2>When I get lucky and my hobby becomes a job</h2>
      <p>
        At around sixteen, I got my first programming job from a man who ran a
        stamp-collecting website. He paid me minimum wage by the hour to update
        the site and work on its checkout process. When I was eighteen, I got my
        first job at a dot-com, Rentals.com, about an hour and a half from where
        I grew up. I moved to Silicon Valley for the first time. Around that
        same time, I met a girl at a party who was later able to find me online.
        Five years later, she became my wife.
      </p>
      <p>
        After Rentals.com and several other startups, I joined Tellme Networks.
        Before mobile speech applications were common, I traveled around the
        country working with major telephone carriers to automate 411 directory
        assistance. Later, I helped build what was one of the first mobile
        speech-recognition applications for BlackBerry. Microsoft eventually
        acquired Tellme, which was a little ironic considering my earlier
        history with Comic Chat.
      </p>
      <p>
        At Microsoft, I worked on speech experiences across a wide range of
        products, including Bing Voice, Xbox One, and several Windows speech
        features. More recently, I helped launch the OpenAI Realtime API on
        Azure, and I am currently involved in other AI projects.
      </p>
      <p>
        I love computers and everything they have brought into my life. I love
        the art of engineering. I love solving problems, exploring unfamiliar
        layers of a system, and understanding how the pieces fit together. Most
        of all, I love the people and communities that technology has connected
        me to.
      </p>
      <p>
        My current fascination is large language models. I have been waiting a
        long time for computing to reach this point, where a person can finally
        interact with a machine in the way I instinctively tried to when I first
        sat down at that 286. I could not be more excited about where we are or
        where we are going.
      </p>
      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <p>
        My projects and code live on
        <a href={SITE.githubUrl} target="_blank" rel="me noopener noreferrer">
          GitHub <span class="sr-only">(opens in a new tab)</span>
        </a>.
      </p>
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        This site uses Cloudflare Web Analytics for aggregate traffic and
        performance measurements. It does not use analytics cookies or track
        visitors across sites.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
